137-### Phase 5.4 utility nodes (frozen v1 semantics)
138-
139-- `story.generate` (Story Generator): in `trigger:control?`, `settings:project_settings?`; out
140-  `control:control`, `script:script`. Configuration is `preset_style`, `story_category`,
141-  `duration` (15-180 seconds), `language`, optional `language_level`, optional `story_tone`,
142-  optional `idea`, and optional `webhook_url`. Explicit node configuration wins over incoming
143-  `settings.style`/`settings.tone`. It calls the importable `studio.story` prompt, webhook,
144-  parser, persistence, and history services directly (never its Flask route), writes
145-  `output/stories/{execution_project_id}/story.json`, and emits the parsed `story_text` on the
146-  existing `script` port. It is provider-dependent, retryable, and non-deterministic.
147-- `utility.set_value` (Set Value): in `trigger:control?`, `value:generic_json?`; out
148-  `control:control`, `value:generic_json`. Configuration `value` is any bounded JSON value.
149-  The configured value always replaces the optional input; the input exists only to sequence
150-  and branch the node. The node is instant, deterministic, and side-effect free.
151:- `utility.condition` (Condition): in `trigger:control?`, `value:generic_json`; out
152-  `true:generic_json?`, `false:generic_json?`. Configuration `operator` is one of
153-  `truthy|falsy|equals|not_equals|contains`; `compare_to` is used by the last three operators.
154-  Exactly one output port is present at runtime and carries the input value unchanged. The
155-  other output is deliberately inactive, not `null`, not an error, and not a success token.
156-  `contains` means membership for arrays/object keys and substring containment for strings;
157-  other input types evaluate false. Equality uses normal JSON structural equality. The node
158-  is instant, deterministic, and side-effect free.
159-- `utility.wait` (Wait): in `trigger:control?`, `value:generic_json?`; out `control:control`,
160-  `value:generic_json`. Configuration `delay_ms` is an integer from 0 through 300000. It emits
161-  its input unchanged (or JSON `null` when absent) after the delay, checks cancellation in
162-  intervals no longer than 50 ms, and creates no artifacts. It is deterministic but is never
163-  cache-reused because the delay itself is its intended side effect.
164:- `utility.merge` (Merge): in `values:generic_json` with `multiple:true`; out
165-  `control:control`, `value:generic_json`. It is the only skip-tolerant join in v1. The
166-  scheduler waits until every connected predecessor is terminal, then discards inactive
167:  edges caused by Condition/skip propagation and runs Merge when at least one value edge is
168:  active. Zero active inputs skips Merge. Active values retain saved edge order. Configuration
169-  `mode=array` emits the ordered list, `mode=first` emits its first item, and `mode=object`
170-  shallow-merges objects from left to right (later keys win) and fails if an active value is
171:  not an object. Merge is instant, deterministic, and side-effect free.
172-
173-Scheduler rule for conditional branches: a succeeded node activates only output ports actually
174-present in its output mapping. A node with any inactive normal predecessor is skipped, and that
175:skip propagates through ordinary descendants. This is a normal successful-run state. Merge is
176-the explicit convergence boundary: inactive/skipped predecessors count as resolved rather than
177-as required active inputs. Failures and cancellations are not converted to inactive branches;
178-the existing error policy remains authoritative.
