## Findings

- [P1] Copy/duplicate does not rewrite expression node IDs. [workflow.js](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/stores/workflow.js:1243) remaps node IDs and edge endpoints, but leaves configuration strings such as `{{ nodes.source.outputs.value }}` unchanged. Duplicating or pasting an expression-bearing subgraph therefore references the original node, a missing node, or—worst case—an unrelated node with the old ID. Expression references must be rewritten using the same ID map.

- [P1] Expressions cannot be used for dynamic `port_type`, despite the frozen contract explicitly permitting structural configuration expressions. [_port_type()](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/validation.py:116) treats the literal expression string as the port type during edge validation, producing `PORT_TYPE_MISMATCH` and `MISSING_REQUIRED_INPUT` before resolution. I reproduced this with `port_type: "{{ variables.kind }}"` where `variables.kind` was `"generic_json"`.

- [P1] Runtime expression-resolution failures bypass node failure handling. [scheduler.py](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/scheduler.py:482) resolves and validates configuration before entering the adapter exception/error-policy loop. An unavailable conditional output or resolved type mismatch escapes to `ExecutionManager`, which marks only the overall execution failed; the affected node remains idle, has no node-level error, and cannot participate correctly in retry/skip policies or “retry failed node” UX.

- [P2] Workflow-variable edits cannot be undone. [commandSnapshot()](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/stores/workflow.js:103) omits `variables`, although [updateVariables()](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/stores/workflow.js:1187) creates an undo command. Undo only restores dirty/stale metadata; the variable values remain changed. If the document was already dirty and no stale state changes, the edit may not create a command at all.

- [P2] Replace considers same-named configuration fields compatible without comparing their schemas. [workflow.js](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/stores/workflow.js:1324) copies fields based only on name. For example, replacing Story Generator with Animator preserves numeric `duration: 45` into Animator’s options field, and replacing Animator with Music preserves invalid `mode: "video"`. The replacement completes without warning but leaves an invalid workflow.

- [P2] The connection-drop palette advertises dynamic non-testing nodes that cannot actually be inserted. [compatibleInsertions()](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/fragments.js:49) treats every dynamic port as compatible, but [insertNodeAndConnect()](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/stores/workflow.js:1356) sets `port_type` only for nodes in the Testing category. Selecting Workflow Output from an audio/video/etc. connection therefore retains its `generic_json` default and insertion fails.

- [P2] Condition equality is not type-strict JSON equality. [utilities.py](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/adapters/utilities.py:34) uses Python `==`, so `true` equals `1`, `false` equals `0`, and list membership has the same cross-type behavior. Those values are distinct JSON types and can route a workflow down the wrong branch.

